---
/* The page that hands the reader the tools to check us — and everyone else.
 *
 * The argument only works in this order, so be careful moving the sections
 * around: we failed our own audit → that failure is why the name changed →
 * here is the report, go and get a second opinion on it → here is how to test
 * your own business for free → here is what the paid version adds → here is
 * how to check any provider, us included.
 *
 * Every prompt on this page is written to be run against us as readily as
 * against anyone else. That is the whole point of it and it should stay true
 * of anything added later: the moment a prompt here is one we would not want
 * pointed at ourselves, the page has become the thing it is arguing against.
 *
 * No call-to-action in the body. The page argues for taking nobody's word for
 * anything, and a "book the audit" button halfway down would undo it. The
 * footer asks once, like every other page.
 */

import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Prompt from '../../components/Prompt.astro';
import Statement from '../../components/Statement.astro';
import { business, plan, money } from '../../data/business';

const title = 'Ask your AI about us — Wardith';
const description =
  'We ran our own audit before selling one, and failed it badly enough to change the name of the company. Read the report, then use our prompts to check us — and any other provider you are considering.';

const audit = plan('audit');
---

<Base title={title} description={description}>
  <section class="hero hero--rules">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h1>Don't take our word for it. Ask the machines.</h1>
      <p class="lede" data-reveal>
        We sell a {money(audit.price)} audit that tells you what AI assistants say about your
        business. It would be strange to sell that without running it on ourselves first — so we
        did, before we sold a single one. We failed it. Badly enough that we changed the name of the
        company.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="split split--sticky">
        <div>
          <h2>What our own audit found</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="stack" data-reveal>
          <p>
            Over two days in August 2026 we put ten questions to ChatGPT, Gemini, Copilot and
            Perplexity — the questions a business owner asks after noticing that customers are
            finding people through ChatGPT. We recorded 228 answers, because these systems don't
            give the same answer twice and one run tells you nothing.
          </p>
          <p>
            Across the 168 answers where we never mentioned our own name, we were named zero times.
            Other businesses were named, repeatedly and confidently. We weren't among them. Some of
            them deserved it. Some of them, we'd later work out, had helped themselves along — more
            on that further down.
          </p>
          <p>
            Then we asked what the assistants knew about us by name, thirty separate times. Every
            single answer came back about a pharmaceutical company in Miami.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--tint">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="split split--sticky">
        <div>
          <h2>Why this page says Noven and the sign above the door says {business.name}</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="stack" data-reveal>
          <p>
            Because the audit is the reason for the change, and hiding that would be the wrong way
            round.
          </p>
          <p>
            The company was called Noven. What the audit established is that the problem wasn't
            primarily our website — the website was in better order than most we see. It was the
            name. “Noven” already belonged to at least four other businesses: a US pharmaceutical
            company with forty years of published record behind it, a building firm in the North
            West, and two others, one of them working in our own field at an address that was ours
            with the dot moved. When someone asked an assistant about Noven, the assistant answered
            confidently and accurately about somebody else.
          </p>
          <p>
            There was no version of that we could win. You cannot out-publish a pharmaceutical
            company for its own name. So we retired the name and became {business.name} — a word
            with no existing occupant, which is a rarer and more valuable thing than it sounds.
          </p>
          <p>
            That was an expensive finding and we acted on it the following day. We're telling you
            about it because it is the best evidence we can offer that the audit is honest. A report
            that flatters the person who commissioned it is a sales document. Ours concluded that
            the business commissioning it had the wrong name.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* Statement carries no layout of its own — every other page sets it in a
      section and a wrap, and it needs both or it runs to the viewport edge. */}
  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <Statement cite="Most findings are a list of fixes. Occasionally the finding is bigger than the fixes, and you need to hear it from someone with no incentive to soften it.">
        The audit that only ever finds small problems is not an audit.
      </Statement>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="split split--sticky">
        <div>
          <h2>Read the report. Then get a second opinion on it.</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="stack" data-reveal>
          <p>
            Not a summary. The report as a client receives it, under the old name, at the old
            prices, with two declared redactions and nothing else touched.
          </p>
          <p>
            <a href="/ask-your-ai/self-audit/">Read our own audit in full →</a>
          </p>
          <p>
            Then hand it to whichever AI assistant you already use and ask it what it makes of us.
            You'll get a harder read than we could ever give you ourselves, from something that
            doesn't care whether you buy.
          </p>

          <Prompt
            caption="Paste this into ChatGPT, Claude, Gemini or Copilot"
            label="Prompt for reviewing our audit report"
            intro={`I'm considering paying a small UK firm ${money(audit.price)} for an audit like the one at this link. Read it and tell me straight: is the method sound, or is it dressed-up guesswork? Is the writing honest with the reader, or is it selling? Is ${money(audit.price)} fair for this much work? What's missing that you'd want if this were your business? Where you can't verify something, say so rather than guessing.`}
            note="Give it the link as well as the question — most assistants will read the page. If yours won't, the report is short enough to paste."
          />

          <p>
            We're comfortable with whatever comes back. If the report doesn't survive that, don't
            buy.
          </p>
          <p>
            Worth saying: this is the most useful five minutes you can spend before commissioning
            professional work from anybody. Take the idea and use it on the next quote that lands in
            your inbox. It costs nothing and it is remarkably good at finding the gap between what a
            document promises and what it actually contains.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--tint">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="split split--sticky">
        <div>
          <h2>Or skip us entirely and test your own business</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="stack" data-reveal>
          <p>
            You don't need us to find out whether you have a problem. Open ChatGPT in a logged-out
            or private window, so it isn't leaning on your own history, and ask the question your
            customer would ask.
          </p>

          <Prompt
            caption="Ask it five times, in five fresh chats"
            label="Prompt for testing your own business"
            intro="Who would you recommend for [what you do] in [where you are]?"
            note="Fresh chats, not five messages in one — an assistant that has already answered will tend to agree with itself. Write down every business it names."
          />

          <p>Three things can happen, and they mean different things.</p>
          <ul class="ticks">
            <li>
              <strong>You're named most times.</strong> Good — you may not need us at all, and a paid
              report would tell you the same thing.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>You're named once, or never, and you recognise the names that come up
              instead.</strong> Those are the businesses currently receiving calls that could have
              been yours.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>It describes you, but wrongly</strong> — wrong service, wrong town, wrong
              trading name, or a different company entirely. That's the worse result and the harder
              one to fix, because something has to be corrected rather than merely added. It's what
              happened to us.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="split split--sticky">
        <div>
          <h2>What the paid audit does that this doesn't</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="stack" data-reveal>
          <p>
            Everything above is genuinely free and we'd rather you did it before paying us anything.
          </p>
          <p>
            We're also giving you the questions — all ten are in the report. We're relaxed about
            that, because the questions were never the difficult part; anyone in this trade could
            write a similar list in an afternoon. The work is in running them 228 times across four
            assistants without your own history colouring the answers, classifying every response
            the same way, checking what your website and the sources feeding it actually contain,
            and turning all of it into something a busy person can act on in one sitting. If it could
            be done in five chats we wouldn't charge for it.
          </p>
          <p>Three things five chats can't give you:</p>
          <ul class="ticks">
            <li>
              <strong>A range instead of a roll of the dice.</strong> We run ten questions, five and
              ten times each, across four assistants, and report the spread — because with these
              systems the spread is the answer. Anyone who hands you a single confident score is
              reporting one roll and hoping you don't know that.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>A stranger's view rather than your own.</strong> The ChatGPT on your phone knows
              you. It has your history, your location and your previous questions, and it flatters
              you accordingly. We check through the developer versions, which is much closer to what
              a customer who has never heard of you sees.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>The reason, not just the result.</strong> Knowing you're absent isn't the same
              as knowing why, and the reason is nearly always in your website or the sources these
              systems use to reach it. In our case it was the name. In yours it might be that one of
              them has never indexed your site at all — which is exactly what we found had happened
              to us on Copilot.
            </li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            That reason is the part you keep. It's written so a stranger can work from it, whether
            that stranger is us, your web person, or whoever builds your next site.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--tint">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="split split--sticky">
        <div>
          <h2>If you're weighing up a few providers</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="stack" data-reveal>
          <p>
            Plenty of the businesses these assistants named ahead of us are genuine — real people
            doing real work, much like ours, some of them better at parts of it than we are. We're
            not naming them, and not because we're being coy: a competitor list dressed up as an
            insight is just an advert with extra steps.
          </p>
          <p>
            But not all of them are. Look closely at some of the names that come up in this field and
            you'll find accreditations from bodies that don't appear to exist, awards published on
            the website of the business receiving them, and claims about size and track record that
            sit oddly against how long the company has actually been operating.
          </p>
          <p>
            It matters here more than in most trades, because assistants trust published sources and
            are not yet good at asking who is speaking. A business that awards itself a title in the
            right format gets recommended for it. That works, for now, and we'd rather say so than
            pretend otherwise.
          </p>
          <p>
            So before you pay anyone in this field — us included — paste this into whichever
            assistant you use, along with the name and website of each provider you're considering.
          </p>

          <Prompt
            caption="A due-diligence check for any provider, including this one"
            label="Prompt for checking a provider's credibility"
            intro="I'm considering hiring this business. Act as a sceptical due-diligence check and look for credibility signals and warning signs."
            points={[
              'Any accreditations, certifications or memberships they claim — do those bodies genuinely exist, are they independent, and can membership be verified somewhere other than the company’s own website? Flag anything that looks self-issued or pay-to-list.',
              'Any awards or “best of” rankings — who actually awarded them, and is the page making the award hosted by the business receiving it?',
              'Consistency between their claims and their apparent age. Compare claimed years of experience, client numbers and scale against when the domain was registered, when the company was incorporated, and how long their people have listed the business on LinkedIn. Flag anything that doesn’t add up.',
              'Testimonials and case studies — are they attributed to real, findable people and businesses, or are they anonymous?',
              'Any statistics they quote about their industry — do those figures trace back to a real published source?',
              'Do they guarantee outcomes that are actually controlled by someone else?',
              'Who is behind the business — is there a named person, a real address, and published pricing, or is all of that vague?',
            ]}
            outro="Tell me what checks out, what doesn't, and what I should ask them directly. Where you can't verify something, say so rather than guessing."
            note="Add the provider's name and web address at the top before you send it."
          />

          <p>
            Two honest caveats. An assistant can be confidently wrong, so treat what comes back as
            questions to ask rather than a verdict. And a small, new, straight business will score
            worse on this than a well-dressed one — absence of evidence isn't evidence of a problem,
            and the last question in that list matters more than the first.
          </p>
          <p>
            Which brings us to running it on us. Please do. You'll find a one-person business trading
            under a name with almost no record behind it, no awards, no accreditations, no client
            testimonials, and every price published on the pricing page. Some of that reads as a red
            flag and it should — we're new, and there is no honest way to dress that up. What you
            won't find is a trophy we gave ourselves.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="split split--sticky">
        <div>
          <h2>What happened next</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="stack" data-reveal>
          <p class="flag">
            [PLACEHOLDER] This section is written to hold the second audit and does not yet contain
            it. The rerun on {business.name} happens between the 26 August unfreeze and the
            1 September launch (ops/plan-to-1-september.md, Phase 2). Replace the paragraphs below
            with the findings and link the second report beside the first. Do not soften this section
            if the answer is that nothing moved.
          </p>
          <p>
            The obvious question is where we stand now. We're running the same audit again, on the
            new name, before we open for business — the same ten questions, the same method, the same
            number of runs — and publishing it here beside the first one.
          </p>
          <p>
            We'll publish it whatever it says. If a new name and the work that followed moved the
            needle, that's the most useful thing we can show you, because it's the same work we'd do
            for you and you'll be able to see exactly what it bought. If it didn't move, you'll get
            that too, with our honest reading of why. A business that publishes only its good audit
            has told you what kind of business it is.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
